A||||86||88/2.8||Any thoughts on this stereo system?||||6||
Z||000000||88/2.8||11-13-2000||10:23 AM||||I've been trying to think of a good mid-priced stereo system and i think i have a fairly good one in mind. I think im going with a kenwood excelon head and try to shove an 8" sub behind my passenger seat. Any thoughts?||||reg||6
Z||000001||Oreif||11-13-2000||11:26 AM||||I am working on putting an 8" sub where the factory sub goes. I took measurements this weekend and today at work I'm going to use Autocad to design it out. Rough calculations show I can get a box .4 cu. ft. under there.<BR>This would be a sealed box. I will be using a Rockford Fosgate sub. You could go with a "free-air" type of sub and port it if you wanted to. Once I get the box designed and built I am going to post all the specs and pics. The sub you pick cannot be deeper than 4 1/4" so all but a limited few won't fit.<P>I have heard 8" subs mounted behind the seats and if you use a pair (passenger and drivers side) it sounds good but 1 8" behind the seats is a little too muffled with a passenger in the seat. If you install a sub like I have seen where it is built into the rear firewall and using a 10" it sounds good but you lose alot of room.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jonny_blaze||11-13-2000||12:34 PM||||ya im gonna be putting approx 900 watts to eitehr an eclipse aluminum 12" or an Infinty kappa perfect behind the passengers seat.  that should hit pretty good right? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||11-13-2000||12:42 PM||||If you've got the room a 12" is perfect. But remember even with cutting the firewall panel you need to use the entire area behind the seat and the box will stick forward about 4.5". You need minimum 1 cu ft of volume for the box. I cannot move my passenger seat that far forward. Also remeber the console is 4" deep so the box will be .5" forward of the console/glovebox.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jonny_blaze||11-13-2000||01:04 PM||||ill get her in ther, no cutting required||||reg||1
Z||000005||baldlobo||11-13-2000||05:07 PM||||how about 2 6-6.5" woofers behind both seats?<P>||||reg||6
Z||000006||jonny_blaze||11-13-2000||05:10 PM||||you arent asking me i hope?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||11-13-2000||05:29 PM||||I think one 8" behind each seat would sound good. But up a little higher then were you would put 12"s. I think an 8" in the stock location is the best choice for a mid priced, fairly nice quality system. Kenwood makes a great headunit. Which model were you thinking of, just out of curiosity. My ideas for a good quality system at a fairly low cost would be as follows:<BR>Any headunit you want.<BR>2 sets of 4" mids in the dash (2 speakers in each stock speaker well) <BR>OR<BR>1 set of 4" mids in the stock dash, and a set of (up to) 6.5" mids in the kickpanels.<BR>Rear component plate speakers<BR>1 8" sub in stock location, amped.<P>The price of the system depends all upon your choice of bands to go with on the various components.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">    <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000008||jonny_blaze||11-13-2000||07:15 PM||||where the hell is this factory location for a sub?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tom88gt||11-13-2000||07:24 PM||||go to right side, face out, do limbo...lean way back, stick head under right dash, look up at right speaker, empty area under right speaker, that where factroy sub go||||reg||6
Z||000010||Oreif||11-13-2000||07:24 PM||||Factory sub is located under the dash, passenger side, straight above the "foot rest" pad. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||robertjay||11-13-2000||07:28 PM||||I installed a Punch 12" up front where my spare tire would normally go.  Powered it with an older 360 watt Alpine amp, and it sounds pretty good.  Inside it is about perfect, not too loud, but outside it is a bit noisy.  One drawback, putting the sunroof on top of it sort of quiets it a little.<P>I figured with my wheels and tires I would have to strap a wheel on top of the trunk or roof, they won't fit in the rear storage, so a spare tire is pretty much useless.||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||11-13-2000||09:33 PM||||I have an 8" Rockford Fosgate Punch sub behind the passenger seat in a 0.2 cu/ft box and it's a tight fit. I see no way to stuff a 12 incher back there. Those Infinity Perfect subs are awesome, but require a big box and lots of power. The company recommends a 1.0 cu/ft box, but that is minimum. A 1.25 or 1.5 sealed box is much better. No way you can get a box that big behind the seat without cutting.<P>My sub don't sound muffled at all behind the seat. You don't need two subs unless you're a real bass freak or just have to impress your buddies. A single 8 incher in a tiny Fiero cabin blends in really nice.<P>I have Kicker Impulse 4X10's and 4X6's in one Fiero of mine and they rock if you put a little extra power to them. They have angled coaxial tweeters that projects the highs more towards your ears. I wouldn't worry about separate component speaker systems unless you are building a high end system because they are quite expensive for good ones.<P>New high quality aftermarket speakers in the factory locations, a higher power name brand head unit, 8" (or 10" if you must) sub in the location of your choice, and a nice amp will sound 500% better than the stock old crap.||||reg||1
Z||000013||jonny_blaze||11-13-2000||09:51 PM||||disbelievers all of you. i will get the 12 in there, i cant believe no one has done it yet.  im gonna be putting either a rockford fosgate power 800A2 or a premier 720 amplifier.  it will be very loud and i will be very happy.  1 cuft ist too big you know, behind the passengers seat, it can be done.||||reg||12
Z||000014||Bugsy||11-14-2000||07:50 AM||||My biggest complaint about my Go-Cart was that I couldn't get any real sound out of it.  I have a JVC head unit, and a JVC 6 disk changer.  I replaced the speakers, Pioneers in the dash, and Kenwoods in the pillars behind the seats...but still couldn't get any real sound....so here is the result<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Thumper.jpg"> <BR>Kenwood 200 Watt amp Bridged, and one Sony Xplode 10" Sub mounted behind the passenger seat.  The only drawback that I can see at this point, is that the passenger seat has  no adjustment now, it is all the way forward.  I guess its a good thing I don't ride in the passenger seat  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>86 SE I'm addicted and proud of it<BR>Kevin||||reg||6
Z||000015||Oreif||11-14-2000||09:52 AM||||A 1 cu ft box to hold any 12" sub first has to be 6" thick minimum. (speaker depth) Then In order to be at the minimum 1 cu ft with the speaker installed (speaker takes up room) and to fit behind the seat, The width would be 18" so the remaining height would need to be about 30". Just the 6" thickness would force the passenger seat farther forward than the present tracks allow. (measured mine, it's 5.5" from firewall)  The height would be from floor to .5" above the rear deck. You would probably be better to remove the passenger seat and build your stereo system in the space. A 1 cu ft box is alot bigger than you think. Also Infinity recommends 1.2 cu ft sealed with 1.0 being the minimum. The smaller the sealed box the tighter the bass will be. Going too small will restrict speaker cone movement (vas of speaker)and the stock Fiero sub would sound better. A 10" (with or without cutting) is great for the bass pounders. 8" is great for the size of the Fiero interior. Finally Baldlobo inquired about a pair 6.5" subwoofers,<BR>If the boxes were desigend right these would sound very good. The only companies that makes a 6.5 subwoofer are Bazooka, Boston Acqustics, and I think ADS has some too. I had the Boston's in my first Fiero. Not alot of boom but a decent punch. To increase the sounds of the 6.5's I'd go with ported boxes.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Alex4mula||11-14-2000||12:35 PM||||I recently installed a Kenwood KDC-X815 CD unit with a Kenwood KAC-859 5 channel amp (40Wx4 + 120Wx1). The amp is big but fits nicely behind passenger seat. Have Pioneer 4x10, Sony plates 4x6, two Kenwood KFC-T203 tweeters in the pillars and a 8 " Rockford Fostgate Punch XCL sub in a .5cu-ft sealed box. Bass is great all the way to 30Hz. Have materials to build a box in the stock sub location. A .3 to .4 cu-ft box is possible. Have part of the mold but need to finish the bottom part. Will do it in fiberglass & MDF.||||reg||1
Z||000017||jonny_blaze||11-14-2000||06:28 PM||||in the spring im going to put either 2 IDQ 12s or 2 infinitys or 2 eclipse 12s in that center console area.  ill post pics when im done to show all of you.||||reg||6
Z||000018||Oreif||11-14-2000||08:24 PM||||2 12's in the console area? Where are you going to put a 2 cu ft box? On top of the car? Or is your center console going to extend from the center of the car all the way to the passenger side door? You would have to assemble the box inside the car to make it fit. We put 2 12's in the trunk of a Ford Tempo and there was barely enough room for a case of beer. Here's what you do, get some cardboard and make a box 30" X 12" X 18"<BR>and see if you can fit it inside your car. Please note that is the MINIMUM size for a sub box containing 2 12" subwoofers. <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||spike||11-14-2000||08:49 PM||||I installed 2 10s in my Fiero.  The box was not the proper size.   Sure they will not go as low as they should but if you do not plan on entering any competitions it sounds quite good.  I was able too go low enough to shake the front window at my brothers place from the driveway, about 15 feet away.  At this volume the sound was still good.  I will build another box soon I hope.  Oh ya I loose about 1.5-2 inches of seat travel.  My next will have 2 12s.  I will have to extend my box a bit which will not be a problem.  It probubly wont have the proper specs, but I know it will still go BOOM.<P>Tks. Mike||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||11-14-2000||08:54 PM||||Wow, Buggy! That is an awesome looking setup. I like it!<P>Oreif speaks geat wisdom, you guys ought to at least consider thinking about what he's saying. Personally, I wouldn't go smaller than an eight incher for a sub. The 6" to 6-1/2" woofers are more mid-bass, which is great if that's what you want. If you can squeeze a 10" in somewhere, that would be perfect. This is way beyond a budget system, but check out this Fiero competition audio sound system.<P> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||jonny_blaze||11-14-2000||10:15 PM||||orief, dont want any flame wars here, but you are on crack. the box you mentioned is 7.5 cubic feet man.  in that space i could fit 2 stroker 15s.  work on your math a bit.  <P>i can easily fit 2 12s considering that they can take a .65 cubic foot box(eclipse aluminum)  so 2x.65=1.3 minimum. i can easily fit 1.5 cubes using fiberglass.<P>i see that i can teach this forum a lot in the stereo domain.||||reg||1
Z||000022||88/2.8||11-14-2000||10:15 PM||||Do you think an 8" sub would sound good if put in a very depth-compact box behind the passenger seat?? Also, would it sound better pointed rearward or foreward?||||reg||14
Z||000023||Spyder||11-14-2000||11:43 PM||||88/2.8: If you mount it behind the seat have it fire forward.<P>I think the mounting of the 8” sub in the stock sub location is the best location for that size sub.  Also the waves wouldn’t be muffled by the seat. <P>I had thought about mounting larger speakers like this:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sublocal.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sublocal2.gif">  <BR>The seat may be forward but the back will lay back far enough to make the scooted up feeling not as bad.<BR>Hey, it’s just a thought, I haven’t measured.  <P>Another note: I would go with two 8s behind the passenger seat before doing a single 12, if we are not cutting the firewall of course.<P>I know it may not be worth a dime, but it’s my two cents.<p>[This message has been edited by Spyder (edited 11-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||SloPonti||11-14-2000||11:50 PM||||i made my own sealed box for the stock location under the passenger side dash and made an 8 inch rockford fosgate sub in there! works great and no one knows it there! i got an 400 watt rockford sub-the biggest they had at the time-not sure if there is a better 8 inch now or not! also i have a 150 times 2 rockford fostgate behind the passeger seat and i have it wired up so that it is 300 into one! i have pioneer 4x10 in the dash and the deh-7200 with remote! and a dei remote start alarm system to protect it all! the best way for the sub is the 8 inch under the dash! it sounds great and is hidden!!!!!!<BR>hope this helps!||||reg||1
Z||000025||Seanh||11-15-2000||12:33 AM||||I think the center console would work. If it did work, they would sound pretty darn good also. I think the best choice would be 2 10's up front or the 8 in the stock underdash location. I just cant help but notice how much SloPonti likes !'s.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000026||olympic||11-15-2000||01:31 AM||||jonny_blaze,<P>   Get the Eclipse aluminum cone subs, you'll love em.  I have the 12" and they recommend 1.0 cu. ft. per sub for a sound quality setup (which is what I used)and 0.5 cu. ft. for a SPL setup.  SO yes, you could fit 2 12" subs in 1.0 cu ft.   Just be sure to bring <B>LOTS</B> of power.  I'm feeding mine about 1 kilowatt and its just about right.  Orief is right about the box thickness though, the magnet on my subs is about 0.5" from the rear wall and the box is about 8.5" thick.<P>   I think Infinity makes a sub with the magnet infront of the cone so it requires almost zero space behind the sub.  <P>   Here's  my subs:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/subs.jpg"> <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/trunk.jpg">  <P>I still have lots of space even with the 2 amps in there.  <p>[This message has been edited by olympic (edited 11-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Oreif||11-15-2000||02:36 AM||||Jonny, No flames but if the subs require 1.5 for a pair of Eclipse (don't have specs on the Eclipse.) But the volume is <B>CUBIC</B> feet not square feet. This is <B>ADDED</B> to the volumetric displacement the speakers take up! Example a box 12" X 12" X 12" is 1 cubic foot. Now add a speaker that is 6" deep. Taking that the cone STARTS at 12" and goes in an average of 4.5". The magnet which goes in 6" is say 4" in diameter. Which is around 582 cu inches of volumetric displacement.<BR>That is just over 1/3 of the volume of the box. So for arguments sake that would give you .6 cu ft of space per speaker. So the box would be 12 X 12 X 24 for the Eclipse If they will work in that small of volume. I have a list of 12" speakers and the smallest space for  only 1 speaker is .88 cu ft per speaker the rest are 1.0 or higher. (would like to see the spec's on the Eclipse) So the Polk speaker (582 cu inches of volumetric displacement) which is .88 (MINIMUM) would require you to make the box 12 X 13 X 24. To allow the speaker cone to reproduce the lower level freq's without cone dampening created by higher pressure would require around 1.2 cu ft. So now the box is 14 X 14 X 26.<P>Also I re-checked the size I posted previously your right it is bigger it should be 26 X 12 X 16 for a box at 1 cu ft using the Infinity Kappa  Perfect 12. (It's a long throw speaker at 6 3/16" It's volumetric displacement is 728 cu inches.<P>I have been building sub boxes for many years and all have sounded great and none have had any problems. In fact a few cars we've done have won IASCA reginal events.<P>Seems you need a refresher course.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>BTW ~ Those who asked previously, The shape of the sub box isn't too important. (Some ported boxes need a certain space/postion for the port) The important factor is the correct volume of space required.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||batboy||11-15-2000||07:24 AM||||Jonny, Oreif is right again. In case you skipped math class that day, volume of a box is length times width times height. Convert 30"X12"X18" to feet and you get 2.5'X1'X1.5' and then multipy the three dimensions together and you get 3.75 cubic feet of volume not 7.5 like you said. Then subtract the volume of the sub. Remember, this is INSIDE dimensions, not outside dimensions.||||reg||1
Z||000029||jonny_blaze||11-15-2000||02:06 PM||||hahaha you guys are funny....<P>30 inches by 24inches by 18 inches is 12960 cubic inches.  now divide by i cuft which is 1728 cu inches and you get 7.5 cubic feet out side dimensions.  you guys obviously dont know anything about box specs.<P>check out <A HREF="http://www.jlaudio.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jlaudio.com</A>  or <A HREF="http://www.rockfordfosgate.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rockfordfosgate.com</A>  and check out their technical or box spec pages and get back to me when you can do simple math.<P>if you have been building boxes for a liong time, you better recall all your designs and burn them, they are wrong.<P>you guys cant tell me anything, you are wrong, your calculations are wrong and you are obviosly not what you say you are.<P>i cant believe you think that box is only 3.75 cubes, ha its actually double.  <P>not too mention, that my cockford fosgate 12" HX2 speaker displacement was .21 cubic feet.....they are very similar to infinity and eclipses size.<P>1/3 the total box size? ha, try a fifth.<P>any stereo questions any of the readers have, please ask me, i dont BS||||reg||1
